window.docContents[240].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--From-conservation-laws-to-incompressible-flow--What-is-formalized___-and-in-what-order":{"contents":"Each solution concept comes after the analysis it needs. The equations come\nfirst: particle paths and the material derivative, the continuity equation and\nits constant-density reduction, the Cauchy stress and the strain-trace\nidentities that turn Cauchy's momentum law into the Navier–Stokes form, and the\ncommon weak-form test data. Then the functional analysis of divergence-free\nfields: the solenoidal kernel, its weak and Bochner closure, the cubic\ncancellation of the convection form on that kernel, and abstract pressure\nrecovery from a closed-range divergence. Inviscid flow follows, from Euler\nresiduals and solution concepts through\npressure as an elliptic constraint,\nvorticity transport and stretching, Bernoulli's law, time-dependent potential\nflow with recovered pressure and initial trace, and exact rotation. The\nChen–Hou Boussinesq formulation contributes its rescaling and normalization\nidentities, a fixed-power similarity reduction, and Poisson inversion by the\nhalf-plane Green potential for compactly supported  sources with an odd\nextension across the boundary. Viscous flow brings back the Stokes and\nNavier–Stokes residuals and their hierarchy, a certified shear flow, classical\nsolutions with pressure recovery, the vorticity form, and the weak and\nLeray–Hopf classes with the energy\ninequality, to which the zero flow belongs. The Taylor–Green vortex and\nKovasznay's wake are certified exact Navier–Stokes flows. The steady Stokes\nproblem is solved by minimizing its energy on bounded domains, and the pressure\nof a minimum is recovered in  on arbitrary open domains.\nFor axisymmetric flow with swirl, profiles in the squared radius give a\ndivergence-free Cartesian velocity, of finite energy under weighted moment\nbounds; when the profiles satisfy the reduced equations, the velocity satisfies\nthe three-dimensional vorticity equation and has a pressure on convex sets.\nLast comes the three-dimensional regularity problem: Fefferman's\nalternatives (A) and (C) on  as two Lean propositions over the\nclassical solution class, an abstract continuation alternative for norm\ntrajectories, and the zero datum as a solved instance of the existence\npredicate.\n\nThe De Gregorio model of transport and stretching in one dimension has its\nequation-level theory here. Its blowup on the real line, the  conclusion\nof Chen–Hou–Huang's Theorem 1, is formalized on top of that theory in the\ncompanion repository BlowupZoo: weighted stability and continuation construct a\nglobal normalized trajectory from smooth initial data near the reference\nprofile, and the physical reconstruction gives finite-time blowup and\nconvergence to a nonzero stationary profile in dynamic coordinates.\n\nThe route here draws on : transport from §2.1,\nnonlinear conservation laws from\nChapter 3, potential functions from §4.4.2, diffusion from §2.3 and Chapter 7,\nelliptic pressure recovery from Chapter 6, the Sobolev and compactness tools\nfrom Chapter 5, and the variational Stokes problem from §8.4.4.\n supplies the fluid-specific kinematics\nand vorticity viewpoint;  and Fefferman supply the\nweak-solution and global-regularity statements, \nthe solenoidal function spaces, and  the nearly\nself-similar Boussinesq analysis.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009From conservation laws to incompressible flow","header":"8.1.1. What is formalized, and in what order","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--From-conservation-laws-to-incompressible-flow--What-is-formalized___-and-in-what-order"},"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--Solution-concepts":{"contents":"Suppose the initial temperature on the rod has a jump. Finite sine sums still\ngive smooth approximate evolutions, but their limit at time zero need not be\ndifferentiable. Requiring the same pointwise calculus at every stage would\nexclude the datum we wanted to study. We need a meaning of differentiation\nthat makes sense before smoothing has occurred.\n\nOur sine mode is a classical solution: all derivatives in the equation exist\nin the ordinary sense. Classical regularity is often the conclusion we hope\nto recover from an existence proof. It can be too much to demand of an\napproximation limit, and some nonlinear equations destroy it during the\nevolution.\n\nA distributional solution tests the equation against smooth compactly\nsupported functions and moves derivatives by integration by parts. If  and\n are locally integrable,  in distributions means\n\nThe unknown no longer needs two pointwise derivatives. The test function\nsupplies them, and its compact support removes the boundary terms. The\nHeaviside step makes the change tangible: if  is zero to the left of\nthe origin and one to the right, then\n\nIt has no classical derivative at the origin, but its\ndistributional derivative\nis the Dirac mass.\n\nSobolev weak derivatives impose an\nadditional requirement: the distributional derivative must be represented by\nan  function. Thus  controls the integrability of both\nthe function and its first weak derivatives. The step function illustrates\nthe distinction: its Dirac derivative is a distribution, but is not an\n function across the jump. Distributional and Sobolev derivatives solve\nrelated problems with different amounts of regularity.\n\nFor the elliptic equation , moving one\nderivative onto a test function gives\n\nThe left side is a bilinear form in  and . This lets\nLax–Milgram apply once the form is bounded\nand coercive and the right side is a continuous functional. Monotone\noperators, compactness, and Fredholm theory extend this approach to other\nequations. The zero-boundary space  encodes a homogeneous\nDirichlet condition by closure of compactly supported smooth functions. A\ntrace theorem, when available on the domain, relates that condition to the\nboundary values of the function.\n\nVariational formulations express the equation as an identity against test\nfunctions. When an energy is available, its first variation can yield this\nidentity. For example, Dirichlet's principle seeks a minimizer of\n\nFor an admissible variation , expand  and\ndifferentiate at . At a minimizer the result is\n\nThis is the weak Poisson equation. The calculation explains what a minimizer\nwould satisfy. To show that a\nminimizer exists, we need\ncoercivity to bound a minimizing sequence, weak compactness to extract a\nlimit, weak closedness to keep the limit admissible, and weak lower\nsemicontinuity to keep its energy minimal. The first variation and the\nexistence argument answer different parts of Dirichlet's principle.\n\nTime adds a further issue. For weak heat flow, the spatial energy naturally\ncontrols  in , while the equation controls  in\n. A typical choice is  and\n, with the dense continuous inclusions\n forming a\nGelfand triple. The time derivative is\na functional on spatial test functions; it need not itself lie in .\n\nAn  class in time has no preferred value at time zero. Lions–Magenes\ntheory recovers an -continuous representative and an\nenergy identity from the\ntwo time-space bounds. This is how initial data can become meaningful again\nafter an existence argument has passed through almost-everywhere classes.\n\nFluid mechanics adds named classes because their extra conditions do\nmathematical work. A\nLeray–Hopf solution satisfies the\nweak Navier–Stokes equations together with integrability, weak continuity, and\nan energy inequality; a suitable weak solution carries the local energy\ninequality needed for partial regularity; a\nclassical solution has enough\nregularity for pointwise calculus. A theorem about one class says nothing\nabout another until a regularity or comparison argument connects them.\n\nFor\nentropy solutions,\nan inequality selects among distributional solutions of a nonlinear\nconservation law. For\nviscosity solutions,\nhow smooth test functions touch a possibly nondifferentiable function from\nabove or below is what matters.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Introduction","header":"1.3. Solution concepts","id":"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--Solution-concepts"}});